# Who may do what

```elixir
Mix.install([{:wardrik, "~> 0.1.0-rc.2"}, {:kino, "~> 0.19"}])
```

## What you'll build

By the end, your authorizer lets Maria do anything with her agent, lets Jon read it but not stop
it, and keeps Sam from seeing it at all. An agent acting through its tools can't forget agents,
and in a chat only the owner or whoever asked decides a call. Nothing here calls a model, so you
need no key.

## Write an authorizer

Wardrik keeps no grants or roles. Every operation asks your authorizer, with a
`Wardrik.Authorizer.Request`: on whose behalf it acts (`principal`), who acts (`actor`), the
`action`, the `resource` as `{kind, id}`, and what the actor knows (`context`).

Your app knows who belongs to which org and who owns each agent. Maps stand in for your tables:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Authorizer do
  @behaviour Wardrik.Authorizer

  alias Wardrik.Authorizer.Request

  # Who belongs to which org: people, and the org itself, for its own agents.
  @orgs %{
    "acme" => "acme",
    "acme/@maria" => "acme",
    "acme/@jon" => "acme",
    "acme/@ana" => "acme",
    "globex/@sam" => "globex"
  }

  # Who owns each agent.
  @owners %{"agt_triage" => "acme", "agt_books" => "acme/@maria"}

  # What anyone in the agent's org may do. The rest is its owner's.
  @shared ["agent.read", "agent.events", "agent.ask", "agent.decide"]

  @impl true
  def authorize(%Request{resource: {:agent, id}, principal: principal} = request, _opts) do
    owner = Map.get(@owners, id)

    if owner && @orgs[owner] == @orgs[principal.id],
      do: allowed(request, owner),
      else: {:error, :not_found}
  end

  def authorize(_request, _opts), do: {:error, :forbidden}

  # Agents never forget agents, whoever they act for.
  defp allowed(%Request{actor: {:agent, _id}, action: "agent.forget"}, _owner),
    do: {:error, :forbidden}

  # In a chat, only the agent's owner or whoever asked decides.
  defp allowed(%Request{actor: {:channel, _name}, action: "agent.decide"} = request, owner) do
    if request.principal.id in [owner, request.context[:asked_by]],
      do: :ok,
      else: {:error, :forbidden}
  end

  defp allowed(%Request{action: action}, _owner) when action in @shared, do: :ok
  defp allowed(%Request{principal: %{id: owner}}, owner), do: :ok
  defp allowed(_request, _owner), do: {:error, :forbidden}
end

defmodule MyApp.Agents do
  @behaviour Wardrik.Resolver

  # Every agent here works for Maria.
  @impl true
  def resolve(_request, _opts),
    do: {:ok, %Wardrik.Resolved{principal: Wardrik.principal("acme/@maria")}}
end
```

It answers:

* `:ok` - allowed.
* `{:error, :not_found}` - the principal can't see the agent. They learn nothing about it.
* `{:error, :forbidden}` - they can see it, but may not take the action.

## Setup

Start Wardrik with the authorizer, act as Maria, and define her agent:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Wardrik.start_link(
    name: MyApp.AgentRuntime,
    authorizer: {MyApp.Authorizer, []},
    resolver: {MyApp.Agents, []}
  )

maria = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@maria")

{:ok, _agent} =
  Wardrik.Agent.define(maria, "agt_books",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",
    instructions: "You keep Maria's books."
  )
```

`:authorizer` is required. Wardrik won't start without one.

## People

A scope names who acts. Jon is in acme, and Sam is in globex:

```elixir
jon = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@jon")
sam = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "globex/@sam")
```

Each tries the same four things on Maria's agent:

```elixir
tries = [
  read: &Wardrik.Agent.read(&1, "agt_books"),
  events: &Wardrik.Agent.events(&1, "agt_books"),
  stop: &Wardrik.Agent.stop(&1, "agt_books"),
  define: &Wardrik.Agent.define(&1, "agt_books", model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5")
]

answer = fn
  {:error, %Wardrik.Error{type: type}} -> Atom.to_string(type)
  _ok -> "ok"
end

may = fn scopes ->
  for {name, scope} <- scopes do
    Map.new([{:who, name} | for({action, try} <- tries, do: {action, answer.(try.(scope))})])
  end
  |> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:who | Keyword.keys(tries)], name: "Who may do what")
end

may.(maria: maria, jon: jon, sam: sam)
```

* Maria owns the agent, so she may do all of it.
* Jon may read it and its events. `stop` and `define` are `forbidden`: he sees the agent, but
  they're Maria's.
* Sam gets `not_found` for everything. The agent isn't in his org, so for him it isn't there.

Lists ask the authorizer once per agent, and leave out those it refuses:

```elixir
{Wardrik.Agents.list(jon), Wardrik.Agents.list(sam)}
```

## An agent acting

An agent's `agents/*` and `self/*` tools act for the principal it resolves to, with the agent as
actor. Here's the scope `agt_triage` acts in, when your `Wardrik.Resolver` gives it `acme`:

```elixir
triage = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme", actor: {:agent, "agt_triage"})

may.(triage: triage)
```

It may read Maria's agent, as anyone in acme may. Stopping it is Maria's. And it may never forget
an agent, not even its own:

```elixir
answer.(Wardrik.Agent.forget(triage, "agt_triage"))
```

The actor lets the authorizer give an agent less than the principal it acts for.

## A chat member deciding

In a chat, a member acts as whoever your app says they are, with the channel as actor. When they
decide a call, the channel adds who asked. The channel builds this scope; here it's built by hand:

```elixir
deciding = fn member ->
  Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, member,
    actor: {:channel, "slack"},
    context: %{installation: %{owner: "acme", workspace: "T0123"}, asked_by: "acme/@jon"}
  )
end

members = [
  asker: deciding.("acme/@jon"),
  owner: deciding.("acme/@maria"),
  bystander: deciding.("acme/@ana")
]

for {name, scope} <- members do
  decides =
    Wardrik.Authorizer.authorize(scope,
      action: "agent.decide",
      resource: {:agent, "agt_books"}
    )

  %{who: name, decides: inspect(decides)}
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:who, :decides], name: "Who decides in Slack")
```

`Wardrik.Authorizer.authorize/2` asks the authorizer as an operation would. The asker and the
owner may decide; the bystander may not.

## Over HTTP

Wardrik issues no tokens. Your app's sessions or tokens are the credential: give `Wardrik.HTTP`
an `authenticator:`, a `WardrikHTTP.Authenticator` that turns each request into a scope with
`Wardrik.as/3`. The authorizer decides the rest.

